  "articleBody": "When you're installing rocker light switches, it's easy to make a few common blunders that can undermine the whole project. One big one is simply choosing a rocker switch but leaving it in a really poor, hard-to-reach location. A fancy new switch won't solve accessibility problems if it's still too high or tucked away behind furniture! Always make sure the operable part is between 15 and 48 inches from the floor, per accessibility guidelines, and adjust for any obstructions. Another frequent mistake is not checking the depth of your electrical box or the conditions of the wall before you start. Some rocker switches might be a bit bulkier than old toggle switches, and you need to ensure there's enough space. You don't want to get halfway through and realize you need a deeper box or have to patch drywall! And, probably the most common error is assuming that just installing a new rocker switch alone will magically fix all your reach issues. While rocker switches are easier to activate, it's the *mounting height* and *surrounding environment* that truly dictate accessibility. If your installation involves rewiring, adding circuits, or ensuring specific accessibility compliance, calling in a professional like HH Remodeling LLC in Denver is definitely the way to go. We can help you avoid these pitfalls and get the job done right.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
